man","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1d29f8fd1762","stem":"Enterobiasis can be diagnosed through three of the following: (2 marks @)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microscopic identification eggs in feces"},{"label":"B","text":"Identification of eggs from perianal scrapings or swabs from finger nails"},{"label":"C","text":"Finding an adult around the anus mainly at night when it lay eggs"},{"label":"D","text":"The use of adhesive scotch tape. A swab on perineal region and examine the tape microscopically for eggs (material obtained early in the morning before bathing or defacation)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b13262847290db58a0e27ae98b90fae69cc44e55"},{"id":"q-6063a33f7596","stem":"Stages on the pathology of Strongyloides stercoralis are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cutaneous; (1 mark) o It is due to penetration of infective filariform larvae (1 mark) o Symptoms involved are slight hemorrhage, swelling, itching, papules, macules, uticaria and pruritus (2 marks)"},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary; (1 mark) o It is due to larval migration through the lungs. (1 mark) o Symptoms involved are hemorrhage, exudates, cough, damage of alveoli & bronchial epithelium leading to pneumonitis & bronchialpneumonia (2 marks)"},{"label":"C","text":"Intestinal; (1 mark) o It is due to adult settlement/embedded in the mucosa(1 mark) o Symptoms involved are inflammation, edema, ulcers, and infiltration in mucosal epithelium, nausea, vomiting and intermittent diarrhea. (2 marks)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ca42f5023037","stem":"Prevention of infection by Enterobius vermicularis can be done through any six of these: (1 mark @)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treatment of diagnosed individuals to reduce source of infection in the community"},{"label":"B","text":"Hand washing before food and after toilet"},{"label":"C","text":"Cutting finger nails short"},{"label":"D","text":"Frequent change and washing of under clothing (preferably after bathing), night clothes and bed sheets"},{"label":"E","text":"Body cleanliness through taking bath especially in the morning"},{"label":"F","text":"Educate the public in general hygiene."},{"label":"G","text":"Reduce overcrowding in living accommodations"}],"priority":0,"