 gnats .........","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"are of medical and/or economic importance because their larvae cause myiasis in humans and domestic animals"},{"label":"B","text":"are in the family Simuliidae"},{"label":"C","text":"are in the family Muscidae"},{"label":"D","text":"breed in temporary ponds"},{"label":"E","text":"are restricted to tropical areas of the world ORGANISM INTERMEDIATE HOST DEFINITIVE HOST MEANS OF INFECTION ENDEMIC COUNTRY (not ) Ascaris lumbricoides Soil- Human Oncocerca volvulus Simulium damnosum Human dog encysted larvae in muscle V. Give two epidemiological factors associated with each of the following. (10 points)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d144fb0cd51db9169fa51685d113537208c7fead"},{"id":"q-62e156e7766b","stem":"Taenia saginata is acquired by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Helminthology","topicOriginal":"Helminthology","subtopic":"Cestodes","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ingestion of eggs from contaminated vegetables"},{"label":"B","text":"Ingestion of filariform larvae"},{"label":"C","text":"Ingestion of Rhabdtiform larvae"},{"label":"D","text":"Ingestion of cysticercus larvae by human in undercooked or raw meat"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above Species | Breeding Conditions Aedes aegypti | Polluted water An. Gambiae | Water in small containers and plant axils Cx. Quniquefascitus | Fresh muddy water exposed to sunlight"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Helminthology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"940dcbf8c0b8ffcd7898c0994bdcb200f37f1304"},{"id":"q-f5c021decca0","stem":"__I__body of tapeworm","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Helminthology","topicOriginal":"Helminthology","subtopic":"Cestodes","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysticercus (cysticerci)"},{"label":"B","text":"Taenia saginata"},{"label":"C","text":"Echinoccocus granulosus"},{"label":"D","text":"Hermaphrodite"},{"label":"E","text":"Praziquantel"},{"label":"F","text":"Schistosoma spp"},{"label":"G","text":"Rostellum"},{"label":"H","text":"Oncosphere I. Strobila J. miracidium K. Neck Match ectoparasite with the associated disease |A |Body lice |E |Lyme disease | |B |Jigger flea |D |Endemic typhus | |C |Mites |A |Epidemic typhus | |D |Rat flea |B |Tungaensis | |E |Hard ticks |C |Scrub typhus | SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Helminthology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3491fc6c821287700cba27dc194c185e1fa01c07"},{"id":"q-da20fd2f27da","stem":"Define the following and give two examples on: (8 Marks)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Medical Parasitology","topicOriginal":"Medical Parasitology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Definitive host - The host in which the sexual reproduction of a parasite takes place - A host in which a parasite develops to an adult or sexually mature stage. Also called final host. E.g human is a definitive host of S. mansoni"},{"label":"B","text":"Intermediate host -The host ( animal or vector)occupied by juvenile stages of a parasite prior to the definitive host and in which asexual reproduction often occurs."},{"label":"E","text":"g Biomphalaria spp – Intermediate host for S.mansoni Bulinus spp- Intermediate host for S.hematobium"},{"label":"C","text":"Reservoir host - An animal species which carries a pathogen without detriment to itself and serves as a source of infection. -an animal host that maintains a parasite life cycle in the wild, and functions as a source of the parasite for humans. -Refers to a host or carrier that harbors disease-causing organisms, without injury to itself and serves as a source from which other individuals can be infected. Cats are reservoir host of T.gondii"},{"label":"D","text":"Zoonosis - Zoonosis is any infectious disease that can be transmitted from animals, both wild and domestic, to humans. -Disease of animals that is transmissible to humans -An infection or infestation shared in nature by humans and other animals that are the normal or usual host; a disease of humans acquired from an animal source."},{"label":"E","text":"g Toxoplasmosis and Cryptosporidiosis"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Medical Parasitology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d287fd01ae68a3bc8859aebfcb63d3048c7acc2d"},{"id":"q-cefe0e44b067","stem":"How do you identify the eggs of : (3 marks) • Schistosoma mansoni____Demonstration of eggs in faeces (lateral spine) _ • Schistosoma.japonicum_______Demonstration of eggs in faeces (lateral knob) • Schistosoma hematobium_:examination of urine reveal egg with terminal spine","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Parasitology","topic":"Helminthology","topicOriginal":"Helminthology","subtopic":"Schistosomiasis","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Parasitology","displayTopic":"Helminthology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5d02867bbbde","stem":"Give an account on the following","type":"Essay / Structured Essay","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b317e8257b3f","stem":"Give a short account on","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"False fascioliasis."},{"label":"B","text":"Calabar swelling."}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","an